@page "/learn/environment-view"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Content for some environments only · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Content for some environments only</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your debug panel runs in the browser, but the server renders it first. Which environment is it in?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>IHostEnvironment</code> is now registered in WebAssembly too, and
        <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> renders its content only in the environments you name. In .NET 10 a
        server-only check already worked; one that ran on both the server and in the browser didn't.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Recent orders</i> page. Outside Production it shows a test-data banner and a small debug panel
        that names the environment. The page is static, but the debug panel runs on WebAssembly.
    </p>
    <p>
        A WebAssembly component is prerendered on the server first, then starts again in the browser. This version
        asks <code>IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment</code> for the environment, the way .NET 10 apps did.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The page, with the .NET 10 debug panel" Url="demo/environment-view/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            There's nothing to press: the page fails before anything reaches the browser. The server answers with a 500,
            with no banner and no orders. The error names the panel's <code>Env</code> property: the server has no
            <code>IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment</code> to give it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>No one service answered in both places</h2>
    <p>The panel is rendered twice, by two different hosts, and each host registers a different service:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Who could answer "which environment?" in .NET 10</span>
        <pre class="snip">                          <span class="dim">server prerender</span>    <span class="dim">browser (WebAssembly)</span>
IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment   <span class="err">✗ not registered</span>    <span class="ok">✓</span>
IHostEnvironment              <span class="ok">✓</span>                   <span class="err">✗ not registered</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The panel injects WebAssembly's service, so the server's prerender can't create it. The exception isn't
        caught, so the request fails with a 500. The orders and the banner never get sent.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask the service that's now on both sides</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, WebAssembly registers <code>IHostEnvironment</code> too, carrying the server's environment name,
        with no extra package. Ask for that service instead, and the crash is gone. Then let
        <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> do the check in place of the <code>if</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">@@* was: @@inject IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment Env *@@</span>
<span class="hl">@@inject IHostEnvironment Env</span>

<span class="hl">&lt;EnvironmentView Exclude="Production"&gt;</span>
    &lt;aside class="debug"&gt;Environment: @@Env.EnvironmentName&lt;/aside&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/EnvironmentView&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The <code>@@inject</code> line is what removes the crash. <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> asks the same
        service for you, so a component that only needs the check can drop the <code>@@inject</code>. This panel keeps
        it to print the name. Here's the same page with the panel built that way.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, with EnvironmentView" Url="demo/environment-view/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: EnvironmentView">
        <Caption>
            Nothing to press: the fix is that the page loads at all. The banner at the top and the debug panel at the
            bottom both name the environment the app runs in (<b>Development</b> on a local run, <b>Demo</b> on the
            public site). The panel's <b>Renderer</b> says WebAssembly: it's running in your browser, after the server
            prerendered it with the same service.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The fix is in the panel, and the page uses the same tag for its banner. Here's the panel:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Client/Environments/DebugPanel.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's panel: the fix above, in full. The <code>@@inject</code> is the line that stops the 500 (its
            comment's "workaround 2" is the second .NET 10 option below); the tag does the check.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/Environments/DebugPanelNet10.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's panel. The highlighted line is the 500: the server can't provide that service. WebAssembly's
            service calls the name <code>Environment</code>; <code>IHostEnvironment</code> calls it
            <code>EnvironmentName</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Environments/RecentOrders.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page: a banner everywhere but Production, and a warning in Production only. The highlighted lines
            are the same tag again, not part of the fix: this page runs on the server, where an <code>if</code> on
            <code>IHostEnvironment</code> already worked in .NET 10. The tag just makes it shorter.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Every choice broke one side</h2>
    <p>You had three options for a component that renders on both sides, and none of them worked cleanly:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write it in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@inject IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment Env</pre>
                <span class="why">the server can't provide it while prerendering</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ HTTP 500 for the whole page</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- + Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions --&gt;</span>
@@inject IHostEnvironment Env</pre>
                <span class="why">won't compile in the client project without the package (CS0234)</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ prerenders, then crashes when WebAssembly starts: the Blazor error bar appears</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DebugPanel @@rendermode="new
    InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(
        <span class="err">prerender: false</span>)" /&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ no crash, but the panel only appears once WebAssembly has downloaded</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        A component that ran only on the server could inject <code>IHostEnvironment</code> and check it with an
        <code>if</code>. A shared one needed two code paths, or an abstraction of your own.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One service, and a tag on top</h2>
    <p>
        The second option is the one that works now, without the package: one <code>@@inject</code> line that both
        hosts can satisfy. <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> wraps the <code>if</code>: name the environments in
        <code>Include</code> or <code>Exclude</code>, and Fig. 2 uses both.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It isn't a secret keeper.</b> A WebAssembly component's markup ships to the browser in its DLL, whatever the environment. Keep secrets on the server.</li>
        <li><b>Names are comma-separated.</b> <code>Include="Development,Staging"</code>. Case doesn't matter, and if a name is in both lists, <code>Exclude</code> wins.</li>
        <li><b>It was called <code>EnvironmentBoundary</code>.</b> It was renamed in Preview 6, so older posts use the old name.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
